gstuhl-reports/volume14/issue02/24062/DagRep.14.2.71/DagRep.14.2.71.pdf)\nciteturn7search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; It follows from Kuratowski’s theorem, that these ... A plane star-forest is a geometric graph in which each component is a star (a ... Conjecture. ... 2024.\nA complete convex\ngeometric graph is a geometric graph whose vertex set is a set of points in the plane in\nstrictly convex position, where every pair of vertices are connected by an edge.\nAnswering a question of Dujmović and Wood [3] Pach, Saghafian, and Schnider [5] proved\nthat the edge set of a complete convex geometric graph on n vertices cannot be covered by\nfewer than n −1 plane star-forests. This bound is tight. They made the following\nConjecture.\nNo complete geometric graph can be covered with less than 3n/4 plane star\nforests.\nThis was proved to be false [1]\nTheorem.\n(Antić, Gliˇić, Milivojčević): There are infinitely many even values of n, for\nwhich there exists a complete geometric graph with n vertices whose edges set can be covered\nby n/2 + 1 plane star-forests.\nWe studied the analogous problem where instead of star-forests, we are allowed to use\nany plane trees. It appears to be true that there exists a constant c > 0 such that the edge\nset of every complete geometric graph can be covered by (1 −c)n plane trees. We verified\nthis conjecture in some special cases.\nReferences\n1\nTodor Antić, Jelena Glišić, and Milan Milivojčević. Star-forest decompositions of certain\ngeometric graphs. In 40th European Workshop on Computational Geometry (EuroCG24),\n2024.\n24062--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngt.geometric topology - Least cardinality of a set of points in the plane - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/90454/least-cardinality-of-a-set-of-points-in-the-plane)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; What can be said about K if all the points of S must be at integer distance from eachother, can it be proved to be finite? ... Let $S=S'\\cup\\{(x,y)\\}$, where $1,x,y$ are algebraically independent over $\\mathbb{Q}$.\n\n–  Boris Bukh\n    9. # Least cardinality of a set of points in the plane\n\nAsked 14 years, 6 months ago\n\nModified 14 years, 6 months ago\n\nViewed 605 times\n\nWhat is the least possible cardinality $K$, of a set S of points in the plane, such that there exists a point P in the plane and an open ball B centered at P, such that for all points X in B, not all the euclidean distances from X to points of S are rational?\n\nCan we do better then $3\\leq K \\leq 2^{\\aleph_0}$?\n\nWhat can be said about K if all the points of S must be at integer distance from eachother, can it be proved to be finite?\n\n  * gt.geometric-topology\n  * geometry\n  * mg.metric-geometry\n  * gn.general-topology\n\nThree points suffice. Take $S′=\\{(0,0),(1,0)\\}$. Then any point with algebraic distances to $S'$ has algebraic coordinates (it lies on the intersection of circles with algebraic coefficients). Let $S=S'\\cup\\{(x,y)\\}$, where $1,x,y$ are algebraically independent over $\\mathbb{Q}$.\n\n–  Boris Bukh\n\n4\n\n[Input]\n\nAs Boris Bukh points out, three points suffice, but I'd like to point out that your question is related to this MO question.\n\nHere is a summary of the information in the previous question. For the second part of your question, the author (me) conjectures that for any finite set $S$ with all rational distances, no such point $P$ exists. As I noted in the comments, this is true when $|S|=3$, proven by Almering.\n\nIt is not known if there is a point with all rational distances to the unit square. However, it is known that there are no points at rational distance from all vertices of a regular $n$-gon, except perhaps when $n=4,6,8,12,24$.\n\nSome more tangential remarks are that it is not known if there is a dense set of points in the plane with all distances rational, although it is conjectured that there is none.\n\nEven more tangential, it is not known if every planar graph can be straight-line embedded in the plane with all edges having rational length, although it is conjectured to be true.\n\n\nedited Apr 13, 2017 at 12:58\n\nCommunityBot\n\n1 2 2 silver badges 3 3 bronze badges\n\nanswered Mar 7, 2012 at 16:41\n\nTony Huynh\n\n33.1k 12 12 gold badges 121 121 silver badges 192 192 bronze badges--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDecomposition of geometric graphs into star-forests - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0925772125000240)\nciteturn7search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; 10.1016/j.comgeo.2025.102186 ... The conjecture that every complete geometric graph on 2m vertices can be decomposed into m plane spanning trees has been recently disproved in [3]. ... ### Covering with plane star-forests–proof of Theorem 1 ... Instead of decompositions of {K}_{n} into plane star-forests, it will be more convenient to consider coverings, that is, to allow an edge to belong to more than one star-forest (to have more than one “color”).\n\nA natural question in this direction is, how much the bound in Theorem 1 can be improved if we drop the assumption that the vertices are in convex position. We conjectured that the above example is optimal, that is, there is no complete geometric graph with n vertices that be decomposed into fewer than plane star-forests. However, recently, Antić, Glišić, and Milivojčević have disproved this conjecture [2].\n\nNote that in the example above, all star-forests had exactly two components. A star-forest consisting of at most k connected components (stars) is said to be a k-star-forest.\n\nIt is also an interesting open problem to determine the minimum number of plane k-star-forests that a complete (convex) geometric graph of n vertices can be decomposed into. We do not even know the answer to the analogous question for abstract graphs.\n\nProblem 2\n\nLet k and n be fixed positive integers. What is the minimum number of k-star-forests that a complete graph {K}_{n} of n vertices can be decomposed into?\n\nAs was mentioned earlier, for , the minimum is . The following result settles the first nontrivial case.\n\nTheorem 3\n\nThe complete graph with vertices can be decomposed into 2-star-forests. This bound cannot be improved.\n\nMany other variants of decomposing complete geometric graphs have been studied in the literature, including decompositions into plane spanning trees. The conjecture that every complete geometric graph on 2m vertices can be decomposed into m plane spanning trees has been recently disproved in [3]. Several notions of thickness studied in [7] are concerned with decompositions of graphs into plane substructures. For many other interesting questions on abstract and geometric graph parameters, consult [5].\n\nIn Sections 2 and 3, we prove Theorem 1, Theorem 3, respectively.\n\n### Covering with plane star-forests–proof of Theorem 1\n\nRecall that a plane star-forest is a star-forest which is a plane graph, i.e., its edges do not cross each other. In this section, in a slight abuse of notations, we will denote the complete convex geometric graph on n points as {K}_{n}. Instead of decompositions of {K}_{n} into plane star-forests, it will be more convenient to consider coverings, that is, to allow an edge to belong to more than one star-forest (to have more than one “color”). This does not change the problem, because by keeping just one\n\n### 2-star-forests–proof of Theorem 3\n\nProof\n\nWe first show that {K}_{n} can be covered by 2-star-forests. The construction is analogous to the one illustrated in Fig. 1. Let V be an n-element set, and let be a partition of V into 4 subsets as equal as possible. Suppose without loss of generality that Let be a surjection (onto mapping). For every , consider the 2-star-forest {F}_{u} consisting of all edges connecting u to a every vertex in , and connecting to every vertex in {V}_{}\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStar-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/star-forest-decompositions-of-complete-graphs/wrk_19591a6824f7aec423cdd26997b545f0)\nciteturn7search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete GraphsIWOCA · 2024Presentation Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphsarxiv · 2024 ... They also provide a specific construction for $n=6$ points that can be decomposed into $4$ star-forests. ...   * Conjecture Disproven: The paper definitively shows that the $\\lceil 3n/4 \\rceil$ lower bound for plane star-forest decompositions of complete geometric graphs is too high.\n\n# Star-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphs\n\nTodor Antic, Jelena Glisic, Milan Milivojcevic\n\nIwoca 2024 · DOI\n\n## Versions\n\nStar-Forest Decompositions of Complete GraphsIWOCA · 2024PresentationStar-Forest Decompositions of Complete Graphsarxiv · 2024\n\n## TL;DR\n\nThis paper tackles the problem of decomposing complete geometric graphs into plane star-forests, a geometric graph theory challenge. The authors disprove a recent conjecture by constructing counterexamples and prove a new bound on the number of star-forests required for decomposition, showing that for even $n$, any decomposition of $K_n$ into $n/2 + 1$ star-forests must be a \"broken double star\" decomposition.\n\n## What Problem Does This Paper Address?\n\nGraph decomposition problems, which ask for the minimum number of subgraphs with a specific property that can partition the edges of a larger graph, are a classic area of research. This paper focuses on a geometric variant: decomposing a complete geometric graph (vertices are points in the plane, edges are straight line segments) into plane star-forests.   * Structural Rigidity for Even $n$: For even $n$, the decomposition into $\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor + 1$ star-forests is not just achievable but structurally constrained: it must be a \"broken double star\" decomposition. This is a significant finding about the inherent structure of such decompositions.\n  * Novel Geometric Constructions: The introduction of \"k-staircase\" and \"k-comet\" arrangements provides concrete geometric configurations that achieve the proven bounds, offering tools for further research.\n\n## Key Results\n\n  * The conjecture by Pach, Saghafian, and Schnider is disproven.\n  * It is proven that for any $n$, a complete geometric graph on $n$ vertices can be decomposed into $\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor + 1$ plane star-forests.\n  * For any even integer $n=2k$, any decomposition of the abstract complete graph $K_n$ into $k+1$ star-forests is a \"broken double star\" decomposition.\n\n## Limitations and Future Work\n\n  * Computational Search: While the paper presents theoretical constructions, it notes that computational searches for small point sets (6 and 8 points) were conducted to find examples and verify conjectures. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Graphs on Euclidean Spaces Defined Using Transcendental Distances (https://www.academia.edu/145899850/Graphs_on_Euclidean_Spaces_Defined_Using_Transcendental_Distances)\nciteturn7search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Bukh conjectures that if D is algebraically independent, then Chr(X n (D)), the chromatic number of X n (D) is finite. ... An algebraic distance graph is defined to be a graph with vertices in En in which two vertices are adjacent if and only if the distance between them is an algebraic number. ... Bukh: Measurable sets with excluded distances, Geometric and Func- tional Analysis, 18(2008), 668-697.\n\n# Graphs on Euclidean Spaces Defined Using Transcendental Distances\n\nPéter Komjáth\n\n2011, Mathematika\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1112/S0025579311001999\n\nvisibility\n\n…\n\ndescription See ful